o [andybunn] read.tucson(): split the negative-value rule. One line,
  tailNums[tailNums < 0 & tailNums != -9999] <- NA, treats a file's
  sentinel (-999, -8, -5, ...) and an apparent sign error (nc030e:
  685 578 651 664 -249 870) the same way, and says nothing about
  either. A value repeated in the file is its sentinel: report it once.
  A lone negative is a suspected sign error: flag it. Both still return
  NA; never flip -249 to 249. Evidence and counts are in the ITRDB clone
  (itrdbMeasurementsClone, project notes, "Reader work still open in
  dplR").

o [andybunn] read.tucson(): decide what to do with a file that has
  exactly one negative cell. The repetition rule above cannot tell a
  sentinel used once from a sign error. 261 ITRDB files, 191 of them a
  single -999. Either report all 261 as undecidable, or treat a file's
  only negative as its sentinel (13 files then get a message).

o [andybunn] read.tucson(): a 999 in the middle of a line in a 0.01 mm
  file is deleted as a stop marker, silently. 21 ITRDB files, 149
  cells. Mostly real markers, but in dza010 (BES04B 1952), bgd002 and
  deu440 it is a plausible 9.99 mm ring. Give it the same treatment as
  sentinels: one message per file with the count.

o [andybunn] read.tucson(): should a line that cannot be read
  unambiguously be an error by default, not only under strict = TRUE?
  Making COLUMN_LAYOUT and PAST_COL72 fatal would newly fail 20 ITRDB
  files. The case for it, file by file, is in
  itrdbMeasurementsClone/notes/read-tucson-fail-on-unreadable.md.

o [andybunn] Druckenbrod disturbance detrending. modendro
  (github.com/ctmaher/modendro, Colin Maher) has ci_detect()
  (Druckenbrod 2013, Rydval 2016/2018) and d_detrend() (Druckenbrod 2024,
  ported from Dan's MATLAB). First simulation 2026-10-20: 200-yr series,
  negative-exponential age trend times AR(1) noise (phi 0.5, sd 0.25 in
  log), one planted release (2-yr ramp, sustained or decaying with a
  15-yr e-folding), 100 series per case, defaults. Sustained +50/100/200%
  found 35/71/99% of the time, onset exact or a year late; 16% of
  no-release controls got a false event, at little cost. The index beat
  a plain AgeDepSpline at +200% (r 0.92 vs 0.67). After an event the
  level is pinned to the single onset ring (d_detrend.R line 744), so
  that ring's noise stays in the rest of the series. Bugs: fails on a
  one-series rwl; a plain data.frame gives "no year column". Scripts and
  results in workspace/modendro-sims. Still open:
  - Redo the sims with sim.collection() from learningToLoveDplR
    (R/sim-collection.R); the first round used ad hoc code.
  - Check whether the 2024 paper adds back the first value too.
  - Measure how much of each post-event offset the onset ring explains.
  - Add a common climate signal with a decade-long wet or dry spell and
    count how often climate is removed as a disturbance.
  - Expand the sims: several events per series, suppressions, releases
    near the juvenile cutoff, other noise levels and thresholds.
  - Run Dan's MATLAB and d_detrend on the same data to confirm the port.
  - Decide whether dplR points to modendro or brings this in with
    Colin's agreement. Talk to Kevin first.

o [andybunn] revamp the plots. Most of dplR's plots were built before
  2010 and need attention.

o [andybunn] negative nyrs as percent of series length, as ARSTAN does
  (its spline option "<-9"). caps() done 2026-11-11 (uncommitted): -67
  is a 67% spline, the proportion form (0, 1] stays, so nyrs = 1 is
  100% of n, the same rule as dplPy's get_period(). caps() stops when
  nyrs truncates below a 2-year spline (0 gave NaN, 1 a flat line).
  detrend(method = "Spline") and the xdating functions (through
  check.normalize.args() and nyrs.rwi() in helpers.R, done the same
  day) pick it up; xdate.report() now stops on a bad nyrs (it
  used to exclude every series); NEWS, help pages for caps, detrend and
  the ten xdating functions, and tests/testthat/test-caps-nyrs.R. dplR keeps
  truncating nyrs to whole years (decided 2026-11-11); note
  for Kevin in workspace/note-kevin-spline-rounding.md. Still open:
  - The dplR.detrend record and the per-series stats keep nyrs as given
    (-67 or 0.67), not the n-year spline it gave. Record both?
